              "text": "(1) For dinosaur hunters, few places on Earth are as exciting as the Gobi Desert. Its dry climate and lack of plants mean that rocks are not hidden under soil or forest, and the wind slowly uncovers bones that have been buried for tens of millions of years.\n\n(2) The world first learned about the Gobi's treasures in the 1920s, when an American expedition led by Roy Chapman Andrews explored the area. At a place now known as the Flaming Cliffs, the team found nests of fossil eggs. They were among the first dinosaur eggs to be recognised by scientists, and they made headlines around the world.\n\n(3) The expeditions also found the bones of a small, fast hunter later named Velociraptor. Films have made it famous as a large, terrifying monster, but the real animal was only about the size of a turkey, and scientists now believe it was covered in feathers.\n\n(4) One of the most famous fossils ever discovered was found in the Gobi in 1971. It shows a Velociraptor and a plant-eating Protoceratops locked together in a fight. Both animals seem to have been buried suddenly, perhaps by a collapsing sand dune, and they have stayed in the same position for more than seventy million years.\n\n(5) Unfortunately, the Gobi's fossils have also attracted thieves. Some skeletons have been smuggled out of the country and sold to private collectors. In 2013, after a legal case in the United States, a skeleton of the large hunter Tarbosaurus was returned to Mongolia.\n\n(6) Today, Mongolian scientists work with researchers from many countries, and new species are still being discovered. Each discovery helps us understand not only the dinosaurs themselves but also how our planet has changed. The desert, it seems, still has many stories to tell.",
